Hexagonal Grids
Hexagons instead of squares:

hex_grid &lt;- st_make_grid(region, n = c(70, 50), square = FALSE) |&gt;
  st_as_sf() |&gt;
  mutate(id = 1:n())

Advantages over squares: - Reduced edge effects (lower perimeter-to-area ratio) - All neighbors identical (no “rook vs. queen”) - Better fit to curved surfaces (Earth) - More human perception-friendly for visualization

Voronoi Polygons (Thiessen Polygons)
Partition space based on anchor points—each polygon is the area closest to its anchor:

# Convert points to multipoint, create Voronoi
voronoi_grid &lt;- st_voronoi(st_union(anchor_points)) |&gt;
  st_cast() |&gt;
  st_as_sf() |&gt;
  mutate(id = 1:n())

Useful for: - Nearest-neighbor search - Facility location optimization - Spatial interpolation (e.g., rainfall from gauges) - Largest empty area problems

Delaunay Triangulation: Visual Example

Delaunay: Triangulates anchor points. Voronoi: Complements it—circumcenters of Delaunay triangles are Voronoi vertices.
Connect anchor points with triangles such that no point lies inside any triangle’s circumcircle:

triangles &lt;- st_triangulate(st_union(anchor_points)) |&gt;
  st_cast() |&gt;
  st_as_sf() |&gt;
  mutate(id = 1:n())

Relationship: Delaunay triangles are the dual of Voronoi polygons (circumcenters of triangles = Voronoi vertices).
Used in: - Terrain modeling (TINs—Triangular Irregular Networks) - Flood mapping - 3D surface interpolation

A simpler (binary) version of this matrix can be created by mapping all non-empty intersections {0,1,2} to TRUE.

Where II would state: “Does the Interior of”a” overlaps with the Interior of “b” in a way that produces a point (0), line (1), or polygon (2)”
Where IB would state: “Does the Interior of”a” overlap with the Boundary of “b” in a way that produces a point (0), line (1), or polygon (2)”
Both matrix forms:

dimensional {0,1,2,F}
Boolean {T,F}


Can be serialize as a “DE-9IM string code” representing the matrix in a single string element (standardized format for data interchange)
The OGC has standardized the typical spatial predicates (Contains, Crosses, Intersects, Touches, etc.) as Boolean functions, and the DE-9IM model as a function that returns the DE-9IM code, with domain of {0,1,2,F}

